  "query_preview": "..\nA monsoon surge continues just south of the Marianas as Typhoon\nDolphin, centered near 23N152E, treks west-northwestward away from \nMinamitorishima. Scatterometry this morning shows an extensive area \nof 20 to 30 knot west to southwest winds to the south of Guam,\nstretching eastward to the north of Chuuk, Pohnpei, and Kosrae,\naccompanying a band of numerous showers and isolated to scattered\nthunderstorms. These fresh to strong westerlies and associated\nconvection will gradually build northwest into the area over the next\nfew days as Dolphin passes north of the area near Iwo To. Windy\nconditions are expected to develop for Guam on Monday and increase\nTuesday as the monsoon surge expands northward across the CNMI, with\nmodels indicating a peak in winds and showers around Tuesday and \nTuesday night. Winds will relax slightly Wednesday through Friday,\nbecoming moderate to fresh as Dolphin pulls away and a col area \ndevelops near or east of the far northern CNMI. Then, the monsoon\ntrough and surge look to shift back toward the south and \nreinvigorate across the area this coming weekend.\n\nThe increase in winds, wind waves, and building swells from Typhoon \nDolphin and its associated monsoon pattern, will bring marine and \nsurf hazards across the region for much of the week, discussed in the\nsection below. Currently, a Small Craft Advisory remains in effect \nfor Guam, and will also go into effect for Rota, Tinian, and Saipan \nat 6 AM ChST Monday morning. A High Risk of Rip Currents remains in \neffect along east facing reefs, but will extend to north facing reefs\nat 10 PM ChST tonight. At that time, a High Surf Advisory goes into \neffect for north and east facing reefs.",
